{"reason": "The initial plan's issues are clearly manifested: validation log-loss plateaus at ~0.354 and then worsens, indicating underfitting from frozen lower layers and an aggressive learning rate. Similar historical experiments successfully used full fine-tuning with layer-wise learning rate decay and lower base LR. The core modifications from the initial plan (full unfreezing, layer-wise decay, reduce lr) are best applied in model_design and training_evaluation. Data processing and feature engineering already run successfully without issues, so they are not modified.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Modify the SpookyAuthorClassifier to unfreeze all backbone layers: set requires_grad=True for all backbone.deberta parameters immediately after loading (remove the loop freezing and selective unfreezing). Keep the classifier head unchanged. This allows the model to adapt low-level syntactic and stylistic features crucial for author classification, as validated by historical experiments where full fine-tuning outperformed partial fine-tuning.", "training_evaluation": "Implement layer-wise learning rate decay: group backbone parameters into 24 layers with linearly decreasing learning rates from 2e-5 (last layer) to 1e-6 (first layer), plus a separate group for the classifier head at 2e-5. Reduce the base learning rate for the top layer to 1e-5 (instead of 2e-5) and use a linear warmup of 10% steps followed by linear decay to 1e-6, replacing CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts. This prevents overshooting and catastrophic forgetting, as seen in similar experiments where aggressive LR caused validation loss increase after epoch 7. Keep other training settings (batch size, early stopping, mixed precision) unchanged."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan's issues are clearly manifested: validation log-loss plateaus at ~0.354 and then worsens, indicating underfitting from frozen lower layers and an aggressive learning rate. Similar historical experiments successfully used full fine-tuning with layer-wise learning rate decay and lower base LR. The core modifications from the initial plan (full unfreezing, layer-wise decay, reduce lr) are best applied in model_design and training_evaluation. Data processing and feature engineering already run successfully without issues, so they are not modified.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Modify the SpookyAuthorClassifier to unfreeze all backbone layers: set requires_grad=True for all backbone.deberta parameters immediately after loading (remove the loop freezing and selective unfreezing). Keep the classifier head unchanged. This allows the model to adapt low-level syntactic and stylistic features crucial for author classification, as validated by historical experiments where full fine-tuning outperformed partial fine-tuning.",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement layer-wise learning rate decay: group backbone parameters into 24 layers with linearly decreasing learning rates from 2e-5 (last layer) to 1e-6 (first layer), plus a separate group for the classifier head at 2e-5. Reduce the base learning rate for the top layer to 1e-5 (instead of 2e-5) and use a linear warmup of 10% steps followed by linear decay to 1e-6, replacing CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts. This prevents overshooting and catastrophic forgetting, as seen in similar experiments where aggressive LR caused validation loss increase after epoch 7. Keep other training settings (batch size, early stopping, mixed precision) unchanged."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution results confirm the initial plan's diagnosis: unstable validation oscillations and early stopping at epoch 11 with best val logloss 0.366. The similar historical experiment (fixing NLTK and TF-IDF alignment) successfully improved stability, so we adapt its approach to refine augmentation and training strategy. The most promising components are training_evaluation (multi-stage unfreezing and warmup reset) and model_design (focal loss and dynamic smoothing), directly targeting the convergence and overconfidence issues identified. Data augmentation is not selected because the historical experiment showed that feature engineering improvements are more reliable than noise injection for this dataset size.", "module": ["training_evaluation", "model_design"], "plan": {"training_evaluation": "Replace the current single-phase training with a two-phase approach: (1) freeze all backbone layers, train only the randomly initialized classification head for 5 epochs with a higher learning rate (5e-5) and a separate AdamW optimizer group, using CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts scheduler with T_0=len(train_loader)*5; (2) unfreeze the last 8 DeBERTa layers, reinitialize optimizer with backbone LR=2e-5 and head LR=5e-5, reset warmup over 10% of total remaining steps, and continue training with same scheduler but T_0 set to half the remaining steps. This directly addresses the rapid initial convergence and subsequent oscillation observed (loss drops from 1.12 to 0.76 in first epoch, then validation oscillates between 0.36-0.49), as similar historical experiments showed that stabilizing head pretraining improves generalization. Implement this by creating two training loops with controlled checkpoints per phase, preserving the existing early stopping logic after phase 2.", "model_design": "Replace CrossEntropyLoss(label_smoothing=0.1) with Focal Loss (gamma=2.0) using class-balanced alpha computed from training set frequencies, and add a progressive label smoothing schedule that linearly decreases smoothing from 0.2 to 0.05 over the first 10 epochs. This directly targets the overconfidence indicated by the rapid loss drop (1.12→0.76 in 1 epoch) and oscillating validation, where the model becomes too confident on easy examples. The historical experiment's success with careful loss tuning (they fixed data alignment but also implicitly improved loss signal) justifies this modification. Implement by defining a FocalLoss class with gamma and alpha, and a scheduler that updates the smoothing factor epoch-wise; integrate into the model's forward method by overriding the backbone's loss computation or computing loss externally after logits."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results confirm the initial plan's diagnosis: unstable validation oscillations and early stopping at epoch 11 with best val logloss 0.366. The similar historical experiment (fixing NLTK and TF-IDF alignment) successfully improved stability, so we adapt its approach to refine augmentation and training strategy. The most promising components are training_evaluation (multi-stage unfreezing and warmup reset) and model_design (focal loss and dynamic smoothing), directly targeting the convergence and overconfidence issues identified. Data augmentation is not selected because the historical experiment showed that feature engineering improvements are more reliable than noise injection for this dataset size.",
  "module": ["training_evaluation", "model_design"],
  "plan": {
    "training_evaluation": "Replace the current single-phase training with a two-phase approach: (1) freeze all backbone layers, train only the randomly initialized classification head for 5 epochs with a higher learning rate (5e-5) and a separate AdamW optimizer group, using CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts scheduler with T_0=len(train_loader)*5; (2) unfreeze the last 8 DeBERTa layers, reinitialize optimizer with backbone LR=2e-5 and head LR=5e-5, reset warmup over 10% of total remaining steps, and continue training with same scheduler but T_0 set to half the remaining steps. This directly addresses the rapid initial convergence and subsequent oscillation observed (loss drops from 1.12 to 0.76 in first epoch, then validation oscillates between 0.36-0.49), as similar historical experiments showed that stabilizing head pretraining improves generalization. Implement this by creating two training loops with controlled checkpoints per phase, preserving the existing early stopping logic after phase 2.",
    "model_design": "Replace CrossEntropyLoss(label_smoothing=0.1) with Focal Loss (gamma=2.0) using class-balanced alpha computed from training set frequencies, and add a progressive label smoothing schedule that linearly decreases smoothing from 0.2 to 0.05 over the first 10 epochs. This directly targets the overconfidence indicated by the rapid loss drop (1.12→0.76 in 1 epoch) and oscillating validation, where the model becomes too confident on easy examples. The historical experiment's success with careful loss tuning (they fixed data alignment but also implicitly improved loss signal) justifies this modification. Implement by defining a FocalLoss class with gamma and alpha, and a scheduler that updates the smoothing factor epoch-wise; integrate into the model's forward method by overriding the backbone's loss computation or computing loss externally after logits."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution shows overfitting after epoch 7 (val logloss 0.3543 then increases) and suboptimal convergence, aligning with initial plan analysis. Similar historical experiments (1 and 2) successfully used full backbone fine-tuning with proper learning rate decay and warmup, avoiding the aggressive restart scheduler that caused instability. The initial plan's changes (linear warmup + decay scheduler, full backbone fine-tuning with layer-wise decay) are the most promising to address the root cause. These modifications belong to 'training_evaluation' (scheduler, freeze strategy) and 'model_design' (layer-wise decay in optimizer groups) to enable safe full fine-tuning.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Add a function to apply layer-wise learning rate decay on the backbone. For each unfrozen encoder layer (all 32), set its learning rate as base_lr * (decay_factor)^(number_of_layers - layer_idx) with decay_factor=0.85, using separate parameter groups in the optimizer. The classification head (backbone.classifier) uses the base_lr (2e-5). This is informed by similar successful experiments that used per-layer LRD to preserve pretrained features during full fine-tuning. The interface: model remains SpookyAuthorClassifier, but the optimizer setup in training_evaluation will use the layer groups.", "training_evaluation": "Replace CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts with a custom schedule: linear warmup from 0 to 2e-5 over first 10% of total steps, then linear decay to 0 over remaining steps. Implement by computing total_steps = len(train_loader)*num_epochs, warmup_steps = int(0.1*total_steps), and update each param group's lr per batch accordingly. Unfreeze ALL backbone layers by setting all backbone.deberta parameters requires_grad=True. This matches the initial plan and similar historical experiments show it stabilizes training and prevents overfitting. Keep early stopping, gradient clipping, and mixed precision unchanged. The validation metric and submission format remain identical."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution shows overfitting after epoch 7 (val logloss 0.3543 then increases) and suboptimal convergence, aligning with initial plan analysis. Similar historical experiments (1 and 2) successfully used full backbone fine-tuning with proper learning rate decay and warmup, avoiding the aggressive restart scheduler that caused instability. The initial plan's changes (linear warmup + decay scheduler, full backbone fine-tuning with layer-wise decay) are the most promising to address the root cause. These modifications belong to 'training_evaluation' (scheduler, freeze strategy) and 'model_design' (layer-wise decay in optimizer groups) to enable safe full fine-tuning.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Add a function to apply layer-wise learning rate decay on the backbone. For each unfrozen encoder layer (all 32), set its learning rate as base_lr * (decay_factor)^(number_of_layers - layer_idx) with decay_factor=0.85, using separate parameter groups in the optimizer. The classification head (backbone.classifier) uses the base_lr (2e-5). This is informed by similar successful experiments that used per-layer LRD to preserve pretrained features during full fine-tuning. The interface: model remains SpookyAuthorClassifier, but the optimizer setup in training_evaluation will use the layer groups.",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts with a custom schedule: linear warmup from 0 to 2e-5 over first 10% of total steps, then linear decay to 0 over remaining steps. Implement by computing total_steps = len(train_loader)*num_epochs, warmup_steps = int(0.1*total_steps), and update each param group's lr per batch accordingly. Unfreeze ALL backbone layers by setting all backbone.deberta parameters requires_grad=True. This matches the initial plan and similar historical experiments show it stabilizes training and prevents overfitting. Keep early stopping, gradient clipping, and mixed precision unchanged. The validation metric and submission format remain identical."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution results confirm the diagnostic of overfitting: validation logloss improves from 0.80 to 0.37 by phase 2 epoch 6, then worsens. The initial plan's three changes (multi-pooling head, longer max_length, gamma annealing) directly address the root cause but were not implemented. The most promising modules to modify are model_design (to implement the multi-pooling head and gamma-annealed focal loss) and training_evaluation (to implement the longer max_length schedule and stabilize training). Data processing requires no change because the current pipeline is clean. Similar historical experiments show that careful loss tuning (focal loss with gamma) and multi-stage training (head pretraining then backbone fine-tuning) successfully improve generalization. The failed experiment's two-phase approach is already present, so we refine it with the initial plan's specific modifications.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the current classification head with a multi-pooling head: concatenate [CLS] token, mean-pooled, and max-pooled hidden states (outputs 3*768 vector) before the linear layer. Additionally, implement FocalLoss with dynamic gamma annealing from 2.0 to 1.0 over total epochs (set gamma as max(1.0, 2.0 - (current_epoch/total_epochs)*1.0)) while keeping progressive label smoothing. This captures distributed stylistic cues and prevents overemphasis on hard samples late in training. Implementation: modify SpookyAuthorClassifier.forward to output pooled_features = torch.cat((hidden[:,0], hidden.mean(dim=1), hidden.max(dim=1).values), dim=1); adjust head to nn.Linear(3 * hidden_size, num_authors); in FocalLoss, add gamma_schedule function and compute gamma per epoch.", "training_evaluation": "Increase maximum sequence length from 512 to 768 for the final 5 epochs of Phase 2 while staying within the original 11-epoch budget. Modify the dataset initialization to accept a max_length argument that can be updated; at the start of Phase 2 epoch 6 (or when validation loss starts to increase), recreate the dataloaders with max_length=768. This provides longer context for long sentences (reducing truncation loss) during the critical fine-tuning phase, giving more stable gradients and reducing overfitting. Implementation: in training loop, after epoch 5 of phase 2, update tokenizer.model_max_length = 768 and recreate DataLoader with max_length=768; continue training with early stopping patience of 5."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results confirm the diagnostic of overfitting: validation logloss improves from 0.80 to 0.37 by phase 2 epoch 6, then worsens. The initial plan's three changes (multi-pooling head, longer max_length, gamma annealing) directly address the root cause but were not implemented. The most promising modules to modify are model_design (to implement the multi-pooling head and gamma-annealed focal loss) and training_evaluation (to implement the longer max_length schedule and stabilize training). Data processing requires no change because the current pipeline is clean. Similar historical experiments show that careful loss tuning (focal loss with gamma) and multi-stage training (head pretraining then backbone fine-tuning) successfully improve generalization. The failed experiment's two-phase approach is already present, so we refine it with the initial plan's specific modifications.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the current classification head with a multi-pooling head: concatenate [CLS] token, mean-pooled, and max-pooled hidden states (outputs 3*768 vector) before the linear layer. Additionally, implement FocalLoss with dynamic gamma annealing from 2.0 to 1.0 over total epochs (set gamma as max(1.0, 2.0 - (current_epoch/total_epochs)*1.0)) while keeping progressive label smoothing. This captures distributed stylistic cues and prevents overemphasis on hard samples late in training. Implementation: modify SpookyAuthorClassifier.forward to output pooled_features = torch.cat((hidden[:,0], hidden.mean(dim=1), hidden.max(dim=1).values), dim=1); adjust head to nn.Linear(3 * hidden_size, num_authors); in FocalLoss, add gamma_schedule function and compute gamma per epoch.",
    "training_evaluation": "Increase maximum sequence length from 512 to 768 for the final 5 epochs of Phase 2 while staying within the original 11-epoch budget. Modify the dataset initialization to accept a max_length argument that can be updated; at the start of Phase 2 epoch 6 (or when validation loss starts to increase), recreate the dataloaders with max_length=768. This provides longer context for long sentences (reducing truncation loss) during the critical fine-tuning phase, giving more stable gradients and reducing overfitting. Implementation: in training loop, after epoch 5 of phase 2, update tokenizer.model_max_length = 768 and recreate DataLoader with max_length=768; continue training with early stopping patience of 5."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan calls for a paradigm shift to a DeBERTa-v3-based transformer model, which the historical experiments show is promising but requires careful training and loss design. The successful experiment (Plan 1) achieved stable improvement by fine-tuning a DeBERTa-v3-large with multi-stage unfreezing and focal loss, while the failed experiment (Plan 2) suffered from instability due to overconfident predictions and rapid convergence. I select 'model_design' to incorporate focal loss and progressive label smoothing, and 'training_evaluation' to implement a two-phase training regime with head pretraining and gradual unfreezing, directly addressing the overconfidence and oscillation issues without deviating from the transformer paradigm.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the single classification head with a DeBERTa-v3-large model from HuggingFace transformers, loading the pretrained backbone and adding a dropout layer (0.2) followed by a linear layer for 3-class classification. Replace CrossEntropyLoss with Focal Loss (gamma=2.0) using class-balanced alpha computed from training set frequencies, and implement progressive label smoothing that linearly decreases from 0.2 to 0.05 over the first 10 epochs. This directly targets the overconfidence and oscillation seen in the failed historical experiment, where the model quickly became overly confident on easy examples. The Focal Loss down-weights easy examples and the smoothing prevents overconfidence.", "training_evaluation": "Implement a two-phase training approach: Phase 1 freezes all backbone layers and trains only the classification head for 5 epochs with AdamW (head LR=5e-5) and CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts (T_0=len(train_loader)*5). Phase 2 unfreezes the last 8 encoder layers, resets the optimizer with backbone LR=2e-5 and head LR=5e-5, warms up over 10% of total remaining steps, and continues with the same scheduler but T_0 set to half the remaining steps. Apply mixed-precision training with GradScaler, gradient clipping (max_norm=1.0), and early stopping with patience=5 on validation log loss. This mirrors the successful historical experiment's stable improvement, stabilizing head pretraining before fine-tuning, and avoids the rapid initial convergence that led to oscillation."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan calls for a paradigm shift to a DeBERTa-v3-based transformer model, which the historical experiments show is promising but requires careful training and loss design. The successful experiment (Plan 1) achieved stable improvement by fine-tuning a DeBERTa-v3-large with multi-stage unfreezing and focal loss, while the failed experiment (Plan 2) suffered from instability due to overconfident predictions and rapid convergence. I select 'model_design' to incorporate focal loss and progressive label smoothing, and 'training_evaluation' to implement a two-phase training regime with head pretraining and gradual unfreezing, directly addressing the overconfidence and oscillation issues without deviating from the transformer paradigm.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the single classification head with a DeBERTa-v3-large model from HuggingFace transformers, loading the pretrained backbone and adding a dropout layer (0.2) followed by a linear layer for 3-class classification. Replace CrossEntropyLoss with Focal Loss (gamma=2.0) using class-balanced alpha computed from training set frequencies, and implement progressive label smoothing that linearly decreases from 0.2 to 0.05 over the first 10 epochs. This directly targets the overconfidence and oscillation seen in the failed historical experiment, where the model quickly became overly confident on easy examples. The Focal Loss down-weights easy examples and the smoothing prevents overconfidence.",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement a two-phase training approach: Phase 1 freezes all backbone layers and trains only the classification head for 5 epochs with AdamW (head LR=5e-5) and CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts (T_0=len(train_loader)*5). Phase 2 unfreezes the last 8 encoder layers, resets the optimizer with backbone LR=2e-5 and head LR=5e-5, warms up over 10% of total remaining steps, and continues with the same scheduler but T_0 set to half the remaining steps. Apply mixed-precision training with GradScaler, gradient clipping (max_norm=1.0), and early stopping with patience=5 on validation log loss. This mirrors the successful historical experiment's stable improvement, stabilizing head pretraining before fine-tuning, and avoids the rapid initial convergence that led to oscillation."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution results show the model reaches a validation log loss of 0.1006 and then plateaus, aligning with the initial plan's observation. The initial plan's root cause analysis (only top-layer features, uniform training) is valid. Similar historical experiments successfully used multi-scale feature fusion and progressive unfreezing with layer-wise learning rates. Therefore, I focus on model_design (to replace simple CLS concatenation with multi-scale feature fusion from last 4 DeBERTa layers) and training_evaluation (to implement progressive unfreezing with layer-wise learning rate decay). These modules directly address the identified limitations and leverage proven strategies from historical experiments.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the current simple concatenation of the CLS token and auxiliary features with a multi-scale feature fusion module that takes hidden states from the last 4 layers of DeBERTa, applies learned per-layer attention weights, and fuses them with auxiliary features via a gated cross-attention mechanism. This captures hierarchical stylistic patterns (syntax, vocabulary, sentence structure) better than a single CLS token. Use a lightweight cross-attention layer with 4 heads and output dimension 256. The auxiliary features (from punctuation and vocabulary detectors) are projected to 256 and fused via gating. Preserve the existing backbone, auxiliary encoders, and classifier interface; only modify how the transformer hidden states are combined. The number of trainable parameters should remain similar (increase by ~2M).", "training_evaluation": "Implement progressive unfreezing schedule: start with only the last layer (layer 11) unfrozen, then unfreeze layer 10 at epoch 3, layer 9 at epoch 6, layer 8 at epoch 9, and fully unfreeze all 4 layers at epoch 12. Use a layer-wise learning rate multiplier of 0.95 per layer (starting lr=2e-5 for layer 11, 1.9e-5 for layer 10, etc.) while keeping auxiliary component lr at 1e-4 and classifier head lr at 5e-5. Apply cosine annealing with warm restarts (T0=total_steps/3). Remove warmup step since progressive unfreezing provides gradual adaptation. Keep early stopping patience of 5 and max 30 epochs. This schedule prevents catastrophic forgetting and allows lower layers to specialize in author-specific patterns, as suggested by the initial plan and similar successful experiments."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results show the model reaches a validation log loss of 0.1006 and then plateaus, aligning with the initial plan's observation. The initial plan's root cause analysis (only top-layer features, uniform training) is valid. Similar historical experiments successfully used multi-scale feature fusion and progressive unfreezing with layer-wise learning rates. Therefore, I focus on model_design (to replace simple CLS concatenation with multi-scale feature fusion from last 4 DeBERTa layers) and training_evaluation (to implement progressive unfreezing with layer-wise learning rate decay). These modules directly address the identified limitations and leverage proven strategies from historical experiments.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the current simple concatenation of the CLS token and auxiliary features with a multi-scale feature fusion module that takes hidden states from the last 4 layers of DeBERTa, applies learned per-layer attention weights, and fuses them with auxiliary features via a gated cross-attention mechanism. This captures hierarchical stylistic patterns (syntax, vocabulary, sentence structure) better than a single CLS token. Use a lightweight cross-attention layer with 4 heads and output dimension 256. The auxiliary features (from punctuation and vocabulary detectors) are projected to 256 and fused via gating. Preserve the existing backbone, auxiliary encoders, and classifier interface; only modify how the transformer hidden states are combined. The number of trainable parameters should remain similar (increase by ~2M).",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement progressive unfreezing schedule: start with only the last layer (layer 11) unfrozen, then unfreeze layer 10 at epoch 3, layer 9 at epoch 6, layer 8 at epoch 9, and fully unfreeze all 4 layers at epoch 12. Use a layer-wise learning rate multiplier of 0.95 per layer (starting lr=2e-5 for layer 11, 1.9e-5 for layer 10, etc.) while keeping auxiliary component lr at 1e-4 and classifier head lr at 5e-5. Apply cosine annealing with warm restarts (T0=total_steps/3). Remove warmup step since progressive unfreezing provides gradual adaptation. Keep early stopping patience of 5 and max 30 epochs. This schedule prevents catastrophic forgetting and allows lower layers to specialize in author-specific patterns, as suggested by the initial plan and similar successful experiments."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution results confirm the initial plan's diagnosis: validation logloss plateaus at 0.3543 with clear overfitting (training loss continues decreasing). The single train/val split and aggressive cosine scheduler with restarts cause instability. Historical experiments show that 5-fold cross-validation ensemble, linear warmup + cosine annealing (no restarts), and layer-wise learning rate decay successfully improve generalization and reduce variance. Modifying model_design to add a learnable fold-weighting layer and modifying training_evaluation to implement the improved scheduler and layer-wise decay directly address the root causes identified in the initial plan. Data processing changes are not needed as the current stratified fold creation is correct.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Modify the classifier to support 5-fold ensemble with a learnable weighting mechanism. After training each fold separately, aggregate out-of-fold predictions and train a lightweight linear layer (input 5 fold probabilities per sample, output weighted probabilities via softmax) using the combined out-of-fold data to minimize logloss. During inference, each fold's model produces predictions, and the learned weights are applied to produce final ensemble probabilities. The base architecture remains unchanged, with last 8 layers unfrozen. This directly implements the initial plan's ensemble strategy and is proven effective by historical experiments where cross-validation ensembles reduced overfitting.", "training_evaluation": "Replace the current single-split training and CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts scheduler with a 5-fold cross-validation scheme. Per fold: (1) stratify splits by author, (2) use linear warmup for first 10% of steps then cosine annealing to 0 (no restarts), (3) implement layer-wise learning rate decay with factor 0.9 per layer from classifier backward (starting lr=2e-5 for last unfrozen layer), (4) train each fold independently saving best model by validation logloss. After all folds, train the weighting layer on out-of-fold predictions. Total runtime ~5x current (~4 hours), within 9-hour limit. This directly addresses the plateau and instability observed, as similar successful experiments show improved convergence with stable scheduler and regularized ensemble."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results confirm the initial plan's diagnosis: validation logloss plateaus at 0.3543 with clear overfitting (training loss continues decreasing). The single train/val split and aggressive cosine scheduler with restarts cause instability. Historical experiments show that 5-fold cross-validation ensemble, linear warmup + cosine annealing (no restarts), and layer-wise learning rate decay successfully improve generalization and reduce variance. Modifying model_design to add a learnable fold-weighting layer and modifying training_evaluation to implement the improved scheduler and layer-wise decay directly address the root causes identified in the initial plan. Data processing changes are not needed as the current stratified fold creation is correct.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Modify the classifier to support 5-fold ensemble with a learnable weighting mechanism. After training each fold separately, aggregate out-of-fold predictions and train a lightweight linear layer (input 5 fold probabilities per sample, output weighted probabilities via softmax) using the combined out-of-fold data to minimize logloss. During inference, each fold's model produces predictions, and the learned weights are applied to produce final ensemble probabilities. The base architecture remains unchanged, with last 8 layers unfrozen. This directly implements the initial plan's ensemble strategy and is proven effective by historical experiments where cross-validation ensembles reduced overfitting.",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace the current single-split training and CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts scheduler with a 5-fold cross-validation scheme. Per fold: (1) stratify splits by author, (2) use linear warmup for first 10% of steps then cosine annealing to 0 (no restarts), (3) implement layer-wise learning rate decay with factor 0.9 per layer from classifier backward (starting lr=2e-5 for last unfrozen layer), (4) train each fold independently saving best model by validation logloss. After all folds, train the weighting layer on out-of-fold predictions. Total runtime ~5x current (~4 hours), within 9-hour limit. This directly addresses the plateau and instability observed, as similar successful experiments show improved convergence with stable scheduler and regularized ensemble."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution results confirm the initial plan's diagnosis: unstable validation oscillations (0.366→0.430→0.367→0.400→0.481) and early stopping at epoch 11 with best val logloss 0.366. The similar historical successful experiment (fixing NLTK/TF-IDF alignment) shows multi-stage unfreezing and warmup reset improve stability, while the failed experiment (adding feature engineering and focal loss) did not help. The initial plan's stacking ensemble is too complex and time-constrained (46 min already used). Instead, I refine the current single-model pipeline by incorporating two-phase training (head pretraining then gradual unfreezing) from successful historical experiments to stabilize validation loss and extend training beyond 11 epochs. Data processing and model design are unchanged because the historical failure shows feature engineering and loss changes do not improve log loss.", "module": ["training_evaluation"], "plan": {"training_evaluation": "Replace the single-phase training with a two-phase approach: (1) freeze all backbone layers, train only the randomly initialized classification head for 5 epochs with a higher learning rate (5e-5) and a separate AdamW optimizer, using CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts scheduler with T_0=len(train_loader)*5; (2) unfreeze the last 8 DeBERTa layers, reinitialize optimizer with backbone LR=2e-5 and head LR=5e-5, reset warmup over 10% of remaining total steps, and continue training with same scheduler but T_0 set to half the remaining steps. This directly addresses the rapid initial convergence and subsequent oscillation observed (loss drops from 1.12 to 0.76 in first epoch, then validation oscillates between 0.36-0.49), similar to the successful historical experiment where multi-stage unfreezing stabilized training. Implement by creating two distinct training loops with controlled checkpoints per phase, preserving early stopping logic after phase 2."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results confirm the initial plan's diagnosis: unstable validation oscillations (0.366→0.430→0.367→0.400→0.481) and early stopping at epoch 11 with best val logloss 0.366. The similar historical successful experiment (fixing NLTK/TF-IDF alignment) shows multi-stage unfreezing and warmup reset improve stability, while the failed experiment (adding feature engineering and focal loss) did not help. The initial plan's stacking ensemble is too complex and time-constrained (46 min already used). Instead, I refine the current single-model pipeline by incorporating two-phase training (head pretraining then gradual unfreezing) from successful historical experiments to stabilize validation loss and extend training beyond 11 epochs. Data processing and model design are unchanged because the historical failure shows feature engineering and loss changes do not improve log loss.",
  "module": ["training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "training_evaluation": "Replace the single-phase training with a two-phase approach: (1) freeze all backbone layers, train only the randomly initialized classification head for 5 epochs with a higher learning rate (5e-5) and a separate AdamW optimizer, using CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts scheduler with T_0=len(train_loader)*5; (2) unfreeze the last 8 DeBERTa layers, reinitialize optimizer with backbone LR=2e-5 and head LR=5e-5, reset warmup over 10% of remaining total steps, and continue training with same scheduler but T_0 set to half the remaining steps. This directly addresses the rapid initial convergence and subsequent oscillation observed (loss drops from 1.12 to 0.76 in first epoch, then validation oscillates between 0.36-0.49), similar to the successful historical experiment where multi-stage unfreezing stabilized training. Implement by creating two distinct training loops with controlled checkpoints per phase, preserving early stopping logic after phase 2."
